H7621":"oath","H4191":"to die","H312":"another","H1288":"to bless","H776":"country/planet","H430":"God","H543":"amen","H7650":"to swear","H7911":"to forget","H6869":"dearth","H5641":"to hide","H1254":"to create","H8064":"heaven","H2319":"New (Gate)","H2142":"to remember","H5927":"rise","H7797":"to rejoice","H1523":"to rejoice","H5704":"till","H5703":"perpetuity","H589":"I","H3389":"Jerusalem","H1525":"rejoicing","H4885":"rejoicing","H5750":"still","H6963":"voice","H1065":"weeping","H2201":"outcry","H5764":"infant","H2205":"elder","H5288":"youth","H1121":"child","H3967":"hundred","H8141":"year","H2398":"to sin","H7043":"to lighten","H1129":"to build","H1004":"place","H5193":"to plant","H3754":"vineyard","H6529":"fruit","H6086":"wood","H4639":"work","H1086":"to become old","H3021":"be weary/toil","H7385":"vain","H3205":"to beget","H928":"dismay","H1992":"they(masc.)","H6631":"offspring","H854":"with","H2962":"before","H2061":"wolf","H2924":"lamb","H7462":"House of Shepherds","H259":"one","H738":"lion","H8401":"straw","H5175":"serpent","H6083":"dust","H3899":"food","H7489":"be evil"},"ccc":{"16":[1063]},"cccp":{"1063":"__P2S#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Me. God answers the preceding prayer, and announces the rejection of the synagogue, alluding to the armies which prevailed in the days of the Machabees. — Not. St. Paul explains this of the conversion of the Gentiles, Romans x. 20. (Calmet) — It cannot regard the Jews, who are spoken of in the next verse. (Worthington)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Gardens, to the impure Venus and Adonis. — Bricks, to the Manes. (Calmet) —"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Idols: to have dreams, (Strabo xvi.) and commit impurities. — Broth of swine’s flesh, which was prohibited, Leviticus xi. 7."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Unclean. Thus acted the hypocritical Pharisees. — Smoke. A just punishment of those who had sought the smoke of human applause."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Bosom: good measure, Luke vi. 38. Rewards and punishments will be eternal."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Hills. Some offered sacrifices to God, others to idols; both unlawfully. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Whole. The good grain is preserved amid the general corruption. (Haydock) — A few of the Jews were chosen to believe in Christ."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Mountains of Judea, (Deuteronomy iii. 25.) which the captives shall recover, as a figure of those who shall embrace the Christian faith."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Plains. Hebrew Sharon, in the land of Basan. — Achor, near Jericho, called after Achan, (Calmet) who perhaps was more correctly styled Achor, Josue vii. 26., and Osee ii. 15. (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Fortune. Hebrew, “Gad,” the sun, Genesis xxx. 11. — Upon it. Symmachus, “without me.” Septuagint, “to fortune.” Hebrew, “to Meni,” the moon, or Queen of heaven, Jeremias vii. 18., and xliv. 17. (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Chosen. Free-will is clearly expressed, as rewards are, ver. 13. (Worthington)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Servants; Christians, (Calmet) particularly the elect. (Haydock) — When the Romans approached Jerusalem, the Christians retired to Pella, and had plenty. (Houbigant)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Execration. They can wish to be preserved from nothing worse. — Name. The faithful shall be no longer called Jews. (Calmet) — They shall be hated, while the name of Christian shall point out God’s servants. (Worthington)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Amen, or “of truth.” False gods shall be neglected. They shall not swear by them, as formerly, Sophonias i. 5. — Christ usually adopted the asseveration, Amen, Amen, to enforce his truths. (Haydock)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","New earth, in eternity, (Clarius) or here indeed, (2 Peter iii. 3., &c.; Houbigant) having purified the former by the general conflagration, which many assert will take place at the end of 6,000 years. (St. Jerome; St. Augustine, &c.) At the return of the captives, the country flourished again under the Machabees; (ver. 18.; Grotius) or rather the gospel changes the face of the earth, chap. lxvi. 22. (Calmet) (Forerius) — After the resurrection the qualities, and not the substance, of the world, will be changed. (Worthington)"],["haydock",20,null,"theodoret","Fill up. To die soon was deemed a misfortune, Psalm liv. 24., and Exodus xx. 12. Virtue is the measure of the Christian’s life, and God will reward those who labour even late, Matthew xx. 13. — Accursed. This age will not be spared. Both just and wicked shall be immortal in eternity. (Theodoret)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","A tree. Septuagint, “of the tree of life,” Jeremias xviii. 8. (Calmet) — Continuance. Hebrew, “My elect shall long enjoy the works,” &c. (Haydock) — They shall not build for others to enjoy."],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","In. Hebrew, “for trouble.” Chaldean, “death.” Septuagint, “malediction.” The children shall not be cut off; and baptism shall secure their salvation."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Straw. People of the most perverse tempers shall become mild by the influence of the gospel, and shall dwell together in perfect concord. (Calmet) — Food, according to the sentence, Genesis iii. 14. (Menochius) — The devil’s power is abridged, chap. xi. 6. (Calmet) — The proudest Gentiles are converted, and adopt the mild manners of Christians, in fasting and mortification. (Worthington)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"theodoret":["Theodoret of Cyrus",0,1,457,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/14570a.htm"]}}